1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,833
Keep four things apart: the outcome we want, the metric, the training surrogate, and the update estimator.

2
00:00:12,833 --> 00:00:21,400
For a classifier: good decisions, measured by accuracy, trained with cross-entropy and mini-batch gradients.

3
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:33,966
The true class gets probability 0.45, 0.49, 0.51, then 0.90. Accuracy only sees which side of 0.5 we are on.

4
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:45,066
0.45 to 0.49: accuracy is stuck, but cross-entropy falls from 0.80 to 0.71. That is a usable signal.

5
00:00:45,066 --> 00:00:53,433
A useful surrogate expresses what we care about, gives local information, is stable, and is cheap to optimize.

6
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:59,766
With a loss chosen, training minimizes the empirical risk: the average loss over the training pairs.

7
00:00:59,766 --> 00:01:06,433
Take squared error and four points. This line, y = x, misses them by these red gaps.

8
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:13,133
Square each gap, average them, and this line scores an empirical risk of 0.045.

9
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:24,300
Another line, y = 0.5x + 1, scores 0.270. Empirical risk minimization prefers the first line.

10
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:32,533
The real target is population risk: expected loss on fresh data. Ten noisy samples; dashed is the unseen truth.

11
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:41,966
A degree-nine polynomial can hit all ten points: training error is essentially zero, but on fresh data it is 3.2.

12
00:01:41,966 --> 00:01:49,500
Training error only ever falls as models get more flexible: a richer family can always fit the samples at least as well.

13
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:55,800
Error on new data bottoms out near degree 3, then climbs. That turnaround is overfitting.

14
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:03,866
Empirical risk can be optimized; population performance cannot be seen. We are looking where the light is.

15
00:02:03,866 --> 00:02:10,766
One remedy is a second pressure: ridge regression balances fit against weight size, with strength lambda.

16
00:02:10,766 --> 00:02:15,333
A small lambda already tames the wild swings between the points.

17
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:23,166
At lambda = 0.1 the curve follows the trend, misses some points on purpose, and generalizes far better.

18
00:02:23,166 --> 00:02:30,300
Too much lambda squeezes the weights until the model underfits. Regularization is a dial, not a cure.

19
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:37,566
So fit the data, but not too tightly. Even then, generalization is never guaranteed.

20
00:02:37,566 --> 00:02:44,733
Training data sets the weights; validation data sets hyperparameters like lambda, learning rate, and hidden units.

21
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:51,566
Sweep the hyperparameter over orders of magnitude. Each point is a full training run scored on validation.

22
00:02:51,566 --> 00:02:59,633
Validation picks lambda = 0.1. Careful: every look at the validation set spends a little of its honesty.

23
00:02:59,633 --> 00:03:07,200
Only now do we open the test set, once, after every choice is made. It assumes test data resemble deployment.

24
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:16,766
A cats-and-dogs model shown dinosaurs: test scores mislead when deployment differs. Detecting shift is only partial.

